            "relation": "HAS_SOURCE_COMPONENT",
            "target_entity_instance": "BEP/BEM",
            "target_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "paragraph": "To represent urban effects, we use the WRF model coupled with the building effect parameterization and building energy model (BEP/BEM)77. In this framework, local-scale processes including street-level airflow, radiative exchange, vegetation effects, and anthropogenic heat emissions generated by air conditioning systems are implicitly represented through the BEP/BEM77 and land surface78 parameterizations, which account for inter-grid variations in urban characteristics such as street layout, building height and density, albedo, heat storage, and vegetation fraction.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "WRF",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "urban canopy parameterizations",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "We employ the Weather Research and Forecasting (WRF, Version 4.4) model76\u2014a physics-based mesoscale numerical weather prediction system widely used in atmospheric and urban climate research. Further details on WRF are provided in Supplementary Information. When coupled with urban canopy parameterizations, the Weather Research and Forecasting (WRF) model enables the simulation of exchanges of momentum, heat, and moisture between the urban surface and the atmosphere, thereby providing a physically consistent framework to evaluate heat mitigation strategies under future climate scenarios.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "WRF",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_PHYSICAL_FEATURE",
            "target_entity_instance": "40 vertical atmospheric levels",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalFeature",
            "paragraph": "The model is applied to seven separate model domains (Supplementary Fig. 11) designed to cover 2,265 major cities across diverse climate zones worldwide. To balance the need for accurate representation of mesoscale atmospheric processes with computational efficiency, the model uses a horizontal grid spacing of 20 km and a total of 40 vertical atmospheric levels.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "WRF",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "DRIVEN_BY_SOURCE",
            "target_entity_instance": "Bias-corrected Coupled Model Intercomparison Project Phase 6 (CMIP6) global dataset",
            "target_entity_type": "Source",
            "paragraph": "Datasets. The model is driven by the Bias-corrected Coupled Model Intercomparison Project Phase 6 (CMIP6) global dataset81 under the high-emission, socioeconomically intensive scenario SSP 5-8.5, which represents the ensemble median within this scenario (Supplementary Fig. 12).",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "WRF",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "DRIVEN_BY_SOURCE",
            "target_entity_instance": "EC-Earth3",
            "target_entity_type": "Source",
            "paragraph": "To further confirm the observed patterns, we repeated the simulations using the EC-Earth3 dataset, which represents the upper-bound case within the same scenario (Supplementary Fig. 12), and the results are shown in Supplementary Fig. 2.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": false
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "WRF",
